        "content": "# Design: YAML Validator Command\n\n## Feature ID\n`yaml-validator-command`\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\n```\nsrc/\n  yaml-validator.ts       - Core validation logic (validateYaml function + types)\n  yaml-validator.test.ts  - Unit tests for validator\n  validate-yaml.ts        - CLI entry point (reads file, calls validator, prints result)\n```\n\n## Design Decisions\n\n### D1: Single-module validator\nThe YAML validator logic lives in one module (`src/yaml-validator.ts`) exporting a `validateYaml()` function. This follows the existing pattern of `src/utils.ts` — a focused module of pure functions.\n\n### D2: Line-by-line validation approach\nInstead of building a full YAML parser (which would be complex), we implement a **structural validator** that:\n1. Splits input into lines\n2. Tracks indentation levels on a stack\n3. Validates each line matches a known YAML pattern (key-value, list item, comment, blank)\n4. Detects errors: inconsistent indentation, duplicate keys at same level, unclosed quotes, invalid syntax\n\nThis is sufficient for a \"simple YAML validator\" without needing a full AST parser.\n\n### D3: Structured return type\n```typescript\ninterface YamlValidationResult {\n  valid: boolean;\n  error?: string;\n  line?: number;\n}\n```\nReturns line number on error for helpful diagnostics.\n\n### D4: CLI follows existing pattern\n`src/validate-yaml.ts` mirrors `src/index.ts` — reads argv, calls the function, prints output. Added as `validate` script in package.json.\n\n### D5: No new dependencies\nAll parsing is hand-written TypeScript using only string operations, regex, and Bun built-in `Bun.file()` for CLI file reading.\n\n## Component Design\n\n### `validateYaml(input: string): YamlValidationResult`\n\n**Algorithm:**\n1. Handle empty input → valid (empty document)\n2. Split into lines\n3. For each line:\n   a. Skip blank lines and comment lines (`#`)\n   b. Check for tabs (YAML forbids tabs for indentation)\n   c. Calculate leading spaces — must be consistent (multiples of 2 or whatever the doc uses)\n   d. Match against patterns:\n      - Key-value: `/^\\s*[\\w][^:]*:\\s*.*/`\n      - List item: `/^\\s*-\\s+.*/`\n      - Continuation/scalar: must follow a key or list context\n   e. Validate indentation: indent increase must be exactly one level deeper than parent\n   f. Track keys per indentation level for duplicate detection\n   g. Check for unclosed quotes\n4. Return `{ valid: true }` or `{ valid: false, error, line }`\n\n### `src/validate-yaml.ts` (CLI)\n\n```typescript\nimport { validateYaml } from \"./yaml-validator\";\n\nconst filePath = process.argv[2];\nif (!filePath) {\n  console.error(\"Usage: bun run validate-yaml.ts <file>\");\n  process.exit(1);\n}\n\nconst content = await Bun.file(filePath).text();\nconst result = validateYaml(content);\n\nif (result.valid) {\n  console.log(\"Valid YAML\");\n} else {\n  console.error(`Invalid YAML (line ${result.line}): ${result.error}`);\n  process.exit(1);\n}\n```\n\n## Test Strategy\n\nTests in `src/yaml-validator.test.ts`:\n- Valid simple key-value pairs\n- Valid nested objects\n- Valid arrays\n- Valid mixed structures\n- Invalid: tab indentation\n- Invalid: inconsistent indentation\n- Invalid: duplicate keys\n- Invalid: unclosed quotes\n- Invalid: malformed syntax\n- Empty input (edge case)\n- CLI integration (optional)\n\n## Flagged Hypotheses\nNone — the approach is straightforward for this scope.\n\n## Requirements Mapping\n\n| Requirement | Design Component |\n|------------|-----------------|\n| FR-1 | `validateYaml()` in yaml-validator.ts |\n| FR-2 | `YamlValidationResult` interface |\n| FR-3 | Line-by-line pattern matching in validator |\n| FR-4 | Indentation tracking, duplicate detection, quote checking |\n| FR-5 | `src/validate-yaml.ts` CLI |\n| FR-6 | package.json `validate` script |\n| NFR-1 | No imports beyond Bun built-ins |\n| NFR-2 | File/function/type naming follows conventions |\n| NFR-3 | Co-located test file |\n| NFR-4 | Strict TypeScript |\n"
